{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"namespaces","title":"Namespaces","group":"Language Basics","order":10,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A namespace is a named scope that groups related declarations and prevents name clashes between libraries.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Declaring and using</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nnamespace geometry {\n    constexpr double pi = 3.14159;\n    double circleArea(double r) { return pi * r * r; }\n\n    namespace units {                      // nested namespace\n        double toCm(double inches) { return inches * 2.54; }\n    }\n}\n\nnamespace app::config {                     // C++17 nested-namespace shorthand\n    int retries = 3;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; geometry::circleArea(1.0) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; geometry::units::toCm(10) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    namespace gu = geometry::units;         // namespace alias\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; gu::toCm(1) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; app::config::retries &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    using geometry::pi;                     // using-declaration: one name\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; pi &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3.14159\n25.4\n2.54 3\n3.14159</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-eye-off\"></i>Anonymous namespaces &amp; <code>::</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nnamespace {                    // anonymous: visible only in this .cpp (internal linkage)\n    int helperCalls = 0;\n    void helper() { ++helperCalls; }\n}\n\nint value = 1;                 // global\n\nint main() {\n    int value = 2;             // local shadows the global\n    helper();\n    helper();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; value &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; ::value &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; helperCalls &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // ::value = global scope\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 1 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Construct</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ns::name</code></td><td>Qualified access</td></tr><tr><td><code>using ns::name;</code></td><td>Using-declaration: brings one name into scope</td></tr><tr><td><code>using namespace ns;</code></td><td>Using-directive: makes <em>all</em> names visible (risk of ambiguity)</td></tr><tr><td><code>namespace a = long::name;</code></td><td>Alias</td></tr><tr><td><code>namespace { … }</code></td><td>Anonymous — replaces <code>static</code> for file-local functions/variables</td></tr><tr><td><code>inline namespace v2 { … }</code></td><td>Members are also visible in the enclosing namespace (library versioning)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Argument-dependent lookup (ADL)</h3>\n<p>When you call an unqualified function, C++ also searches the namespaces of the argument types. That's why <code>std::cout &lt;&lt; x</code> finds <code>std::operator&lt;&lt;</code> and <code>swap(a, b)</code> can find a custom <code>swap</code> next to your type.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nnamespace shop {\n    struct Item { int price; };\n    void print(const Item&amp; i) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"Item $\" &lt;&lt; i.price &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n}\nint main() {\n    shop::Item it{5};\n    print(it);        // no `shop::` needed — found by ADL via the argument's type\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Item $5</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Put your project's code in its own namespace; in <code>.cpp</code> files, <code>using</code> specific names is fine.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p><code>using namespace</code> at global scope in a header</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between an anonymous namespace and static at file scope?</summary><div><p>Both give <em>internal linkage</em> (the name is private to the translation unit). The anonymous namespace is the modern, more general choice: it also works for types (classes, enums), whereas <code>static</code> applies only to functions and variables.</p></div></details></section>"}